Task: Extend docker-compose.yml + add observability configs + Grafana provisioning (Phase 4)

Summary: Added order-service/inventory-service/prometheus/grafana/
elasticsearch/kibana/filebeat as new compose services, all under
profiles: ["observability"]. Confirmed the critical non-functional
requirement holds: `docker compose --profile observability down` (full
teardown) followed by plain `docker compose up -d` starts ONLY postgres.
Added observability/prometheus.yml (scrape config for both services'
:9090/:9091 metrics ports) and observability/filebeat.yml (docker
autodiscover, per-service decode_json_fields to flatten the app's JSON
log payload into top-level ES fields). Added Grafana provisioning: a
Prometheus datasource (isDefault, proxied at http://prometheus:9090) and
a dashboard (purerest.json) with 9 panels covering request rate, error
rate, duration percentiles (server + client), retry attempts by outcome,
circuit breaker transitions/rejections/current-state, and order-service
DB query duration/error rate.

Verification (live, real data, not just config validation): placed real
orders through order-service, confirmed via Prometheus /api/v1/targets
both services "up"; confirmed via Grafana's own datasource-proxy endpoint
(the same path the dashboard's panels use) that request-rate, DB
query-count, and circuit-breaker-state queries all return real,
non-empty results; confirmed via Elasticsearch _search that structured
JSON log fields (trace_id, item, quantity, reservation_id, order_id,
logger_name) are indexed and independently queryable, not just present
as opaque text.

Along the way, found and fixed (separate commit) a genuine Elasticsearch
disk-watermark issue (Docker VM disk >90% used, causing all shards
unassigned/red) via `docker builder prune -f` (the safest reclaim
category) plus a manual `_cluster/reroute?retry_failed=true`; cluster is
now yellow (expected steady-state for a single-node cluster, since
replica shards can never be satisfied).

Files changed: docker-compose.yml, observability/prometheus.yml,
observability/filebeat.yml,
observability/grafana/provisioning/datasources/prometheus.yml,
observability/grafana/provisioning/dashboards/dashboards.yml,
observability/grafana/provisioning/dashboards/purerest.json

Why: This is the core of the track's spec — a locally runnable,
production-shaped observability stack that doesn't interfere with any
existing dev workflow.
